Class & $N$ & $M$ range ($\Msun$) & $R$ range (km) & $M<0.5\,\Msun$ & $N_{\ge0.5}$ & $N_{\ge1}$\\\midrule
\input{tables/tab_audit}\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{table}
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/fig1_data_audit.pdf}
\caption{(a) Mass--radius sequences of the four classes. (b) Distribution of masses; the dotted line is $1\,\Msun$. (c) Fractional difference between the tabulated surface redshift and the value implied by $M$ and $R$; the hyperonic and dark-matter classes agree to $\sim\!0.03\%$, the nucleonic and strange classes do not.}\label{fig:audit}\end{figure}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Class sizes.} The classes contain 222, 220, 122 and 206 models (not $\approx190$ each).
\item \emph{Sampling.} Models lie on a regular radius grid ($\Delta R=0.05$\,km) along each sequence, so neighbouring models are almost identical: for @@nnSame@@\% of the models the nearest neighbour in standardised $(\ln M,\ln R,\ln f,\ln\tau)$ belongs to the same class.
\item \emph{Mass coverage.} @@nBelow05pct@@\% of the models have $M<0.5\,\Msun$ and @@nBelow1pct@@\% have $M<1\,\Msun$, down to $@@minMass@@\,\Msun$.
\item \emph{The dark-matter sequence ends at $0.68\,\Msun$,} so at $M\gtrsim0.7\,\Msun$ the class is excluded by the mass alone.
\item \emph{Redshift inconsistency.} For the dark-matter and hyperonic classes the tabulated $z$ equals the Schwarzschild value implied by $(M,R)$ to $\approx$@@zresDM@@\%; for the nucleonic and strange classes the median deviation is +@@zresNuc@@\% and +@@zresStr@@\%. A logistic regression on the redshift residual alone separates \{nucleonic, strange\} from \{dark matter, hyperonic\} with @@leakZ@@\% cross-validated accuracy (@@leakM@@\% for $M$ alone, @@leakMR@@\% for $M$ and $R$). The redshift is therefore a class-dependent artefact and is not used in any analysis here.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Replicating the earlier protocol}\label{sec:repro}
Table~\ref{tab:repro} and Fig.~\ref{fig:repro} repeat the earlier random-split protocol for 100 seeds. The published 97.4\% is typical ($@@reproMean@@\pm@@reproSd@@\%$), so it is reproducible, but it is not specific to the MLP: a random forest reaches @@reproRF@@\%. Removing the redshift changes the MLP result only from @@reproMean@@\% to @@reproNoZ@@\%. With $M$ and $R$ only, a $k$-nearest-neighbour classifier reaches @@reproMR@@\%, and with $M$ alone logistic regression is at chance (@@reproMonly@@\%).
