\subsection{Degeneracies}
Figure~\ref{fig:cmpop} shows row-normalised confusion matrices. With accurate $f$ ($s=0.25$) strange matter is still identified perfectly (@@cm_SO_0p25_11@@\%) and the hyperonic-like class in @@cm_SO_0p25_33@@\%, but the nucleonic and dark-matter rows remain mixed (@@cm_SO_0p25_00@@\% and @@cm_SO_0p25_22@@\% correct, @@cm_SO_0p25_02@@\% and @@cm_SO_0p25_20@@\% assigned to each other). With a 20\% $f$-mode error all rows are confused with all columns, strange matter being the best recovered (@@cm_NICERpf20_11@@\%).
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/figP4_confusion.pdf}
\caption{Row-normalised confusion matrices (\%) of the temperature-scaled MLP ensemble, pooled over folds and repetitions. Left to right: noise-free, $s=0.25$, and NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode measurement.}\label{fig:cmpop}\end{figure}

\subsection{Dark matter when the nuclear EoS is known}\label{sec:paired}
To separate ``the dark component is small'' from ``the nuclear EoS is unknown'', the observables of each of the @@pairN@@ dark-matter-admixed EoS were also computed for its own nuclear base EoS without the dark component, at the same masses. At fixed nuclear EoS and mass the dark component raises $f$ by a median of @@pairDfMed@@\% (5--95\% range @@pairDfP5@@--@@pairDfP95@@\%; positive for @@pairDfPos@@\% of the pairs) and lowers $R$ by a median of @@pairDRMed@@\% (@@pairDRP5@@ to @@pairDRP95@@\%); the shift falls steeply with the self-interaction (Fig.~\ref{fig:paired}a; correlation of $\ln G$ with $\Delta f/f$ at $1.4\,\Msun$ is @@pairCorrG@@). If the observer were told the nuclear EoS exactly, the Bayes accuracy for dark matter versus no dark matter (equal priors, independent Gaussian errors, mass identical by construction) would reach 75\% at $\sigma_f=@@pairSf_10_75@@\%$ when $R$ is known to 1\% (@@pairSf_50_75@@\% for 5\%) and 90\% at $\sigma_f=@@pairSf_50_90@@\%$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:paired}b); at the points of the precision scale it is @@pairAcc_s0p25@@\% at $s=0.25$ (0.5\% in $f$, 1.25\% in $R$), @@pairAcc_s1p0@@\% at $s=1$ and @@pairAcc_s4p0@@\% at $s=4$, and with a 20\% $f$-mode error and 9\% in $R$ it is @@pairAccf_200@@\%. With the nuclear EoS \emph{unknown} the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes is @@pairNucDM_ALL_0@@\% even without any measurement error (Sec.~\ref{sec:results}). The degeneracy therefore comes from the uncertainty of the nuclear EoS, not from the smallness of the shift alone: detecting the dark component needs both an $f$-mode measurement at the 0.5\% level and a nuclear EoS known to better than the shift itself.
\begin{figure}[t]\centering\includegraphics[width=.95\textwidth]{figs/figP6_paired.pdf}
\caption{Dark matter at fixed nuclear EoS (@@pairN@@ paired EoS). (a) Fractional $f$-mode shift at $1.4\,\Msun$ versus the self-interaction $G$. (b) Bayes accuracy for dark matter versus no dark matter, averaged over EoS and masses, as a function of the $f$-mode error for three radius errors (dotted: chance).}\label{fig:paired}\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration and prediction sets}
The probabilities are well calibrated because the learners are close to the information limit (Fig.~\ref{fig:uncpop}a): at $s=1$ the ECE is @@pu_1p0_r_ece@@ for the raw ensemble, @@pu_1p0_t_ece@@ after temperature scaling and @@pu_1p0_b_ece@@ for kernel-Bayes, with NLL @@pu_1p0_t_nll@@ nats for all three (Table~\ref{tab:uncpop}). Conformal sets have empirical coverage between 0.91 and 0.92 at the 0.90 target for every scenario, with the finite-sample correction applied at EoS level. The price of validity is set size: @@pu_NICER_t_setsize@@ of four classes under NICER-like errors, @@pu_NICERpf20_t_setsize@@ when a 20\% $f$-mode is added, @@pu_1p0_t_setsize@@ at $s=1$ and @@pu_0p25_t_setsize@@ at $s=0.25$. Temperature scaling and kernel-Bayes give the same sets. At realistic precision the data therefore support a short list containing most of the classes, not a single label.
