\paragraph{Within population B.} With whole-EoS hold-out, the MLP ensemble reaches @@B_ALL_0_mlp@@\% without noise (logistic regression @@B_ALL_0_lr@@\%, random forest @@B_ALL_0_rf@@\%), against @@nf_MLP-ens_ALL@@\% for population~A. Strange matter is again recognised perfectly (@@Bcm_ALL_0_1@@\%) and the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes is again at the level of chance (@@Bpair_ALL_0@@\%); nucleonic matter is recognised in @@Bcm_ALL_0_0@@\%, dark-matter in @@Bcm_ALL_0_2@@\% and hyperonic-like matter in only @@Bcm_ALL_0_3@@\% (@@cm_ALL_0_33@@\% in population~A), so the separability of the hyperonic-like family, and with it the overall plateau, depends on how the family is built. At $s=0.25$ and $s=1$ the accuracies are @@B_SO_0p25_mlp@@\% and @@B_SO_1p0_mlp@@\% (A: @@pd_SO_0p25_mlp@@\% and @@pd_SO_1p0_mlp@@\%), and for NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode @@B_NICERpf20_mlp@@\% (A: @@sc_NICERpf20_mlp@@\%); there strange matter is recognised in @@Bcm_NICERpf20_1@@\% of cases.
\paragraph{Out-of-family transfer.} Table~\ref{tab:cross} gives the accuracy of classifiers trained on all EoS of one population and tested on all EoS of the other. Without noise the transfer accuracy is @@X_AB_ALL_0_MLP-ens@@\% (A$\to$B) and @@X_BA_ALL_0_MLP-ens@@\% (B$\to$A) for the MLP ensemble, between the within-population values, and with NICER-like errors plus a 20\% $f$-mode @@X_AB_NICERpf20_MLP-ens@@\% and @@X_BA_NICERpf20_MLP-ens@@\%. Strange matter is still recognised in @@Xcm_AB_ALL_0_1@@\% and @@Xcm_BA_ALL_0_1@@\% of cases without noise, and the accuracy between the nucleonic and dark-matter classes remains @@Xpair_AB_ALL_0@@\% and @@Xpair_BA_ALL_0@@\%. The results that survive a change of population are therefore (i) the nucleonic--dark-matter degeneracy, (ii) an accuracy of about 33--37\% under NICER-like errors, with or without a 20\% $f$-mode, and (iii) the identifiability of self-bound strange matter once $f$ is accurate; the 64\% plateau and the identifiability of the hyperonic-like family are specific to the construction of population~A.
